content.text:
Three findings.

- **13.8 million files** on `/m` (7% of inodes used), for 1.2 TB. One giant `rsync` over that many files is risky. The file list alone needs gigabytes of memory, and a failure loses the whole run. We should sync per account or per bucket from a manifest built from LDAP. That also gives us restartable progress and per-domain control.
- **`/m` is essentially all mail.** Besides `maildirs` it holds only `ldap_backup` and `list_cur.lst`. `ldap_backup` suggests periodic LDAP dumps exist, which we may be able to use offline later.
- **5,843 accounts have FTP enabled** in this directory. That's real hosting, not a stray test entry. When `smtp-mtq-1` is powered off, that service goes with it unless someone migrates it. It's outside the mail migration but a decommission blocker. Is FTP/web hosting in scope for your project, or does someone else own it?

## Next step (one only)

Before designing anything, we need to know which qmail features the accounts actually use, since every one has to be replicated in Postfix or Dovecot. This is a one-pass, read-only inventory over the directory. Run it on `smtp-mtq-1`:

```
slapcat -b "ou=acc,o=ool" | awk '
/^objectClass:/ {oc[$2]++}
/^accountStatus:/ {st[$2]++}
/^deliveryMode:/ {dm[$2]++}
/^FTPStatus:/ {ftp[$2]++}
/^mailForwardingAddress:/ {fw++}
/^mailAlternateAddress:/ {alt++}
/^mailReplyText:/ {rep++}
/^deliveryProgramPath:/ {prog++}
/^mailQuotaSize:/ {q++}
END {
  for (k in oc)  print "objectClass", k, oc[k]
  for (k in st)  print "accountStatus", k, st[k]
  for (k in dm)  print "deliveryMode", k, dm[k]
  for (k in ftp) print "FTPStatus", k, ftp[k]
  print "mailForwardingAddress values:", fw+0
  print "mailAlternateAddress values:", alt+0
  print "mailReplyText (autoreplies):", rep+0
  print "deliveryProgramPath (programs run on delivery):", prog+0
  print "mailQuotaSize (quotas):", q+0
}'
```

It counts which object classes and statuses exist, plus forwards, aliases, autoresponders, delivery programs and quotas. The last two matter most: program-based delivery is a legacy feature with security implications, and quotas aren't enforced on the new server yet.

Paste the output. We'll run the same pass on the `guy`/`gua` directory afterwards, so we can compare. I'm still waiting on which of `quartz`, `diamond` and `andesite` you can reach, and what the legacy POP3/IMAP software is.

